#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203832 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0be03329-844c-5231-a459-314a1182f84b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/id #uuid "1c25c6d9-21b9-59a5-a563-25cb89788286" :question/math-academy-id "e-16706" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1] \\times [0,1] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2x^2y-2x^2+3xy^2+3xy+1.\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at $\\mathbf x_0 = \\big[1, \\: 1 \\big]^T.$ Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $H(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ and $\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}+3xy+1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}+3xy+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4xy-4x+3{y}^{2}+3y \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+6xy+3x\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy-4x+3{y}^{2}+3y) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}+6xy+3x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy-4x+3{y}^{2}+3y) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}+6xy+3x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4y-4 & 4x+6y+3 \\\\ 4x+6y+3 & 6x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n![](images/9a/9a89c30b5dcacbee11ea07f93458a61dd674cc8105af6f0cf5d57898b29b9787.png)\n\nStarting with\n![](images/41/41167d8f07938db9bfbeadb4e672e602d6dfd9c5d0b293a55952b5e8ec7a7c13.png) we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{\\begin{bmatrix}4y-4 & 4x+6y+3 \\\\ 4x+6y+3 & 6x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}4xy-4x+3{y}^{2}+3y \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+6xy+3x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}4(1)-4 & 4(1)+6(1)+3 \\\\ 4(1)+6(1)+3 & 6(1)\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}4(1)(1)-4(1)+3(1)^{2}+3(1) \\\\ 2(1)^{2}+6(1)(1)+3(1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 13 \\\\ 13 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{0\\cdot 6-13\\cdot 13}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -13 \\\\ -13 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{169}\\begin{bmatrix}-107 \\\\ -78\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}0.367 \\\\ 0.538\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203833 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204025 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a19923a-5050-5417-8c83-5fc085a81f0f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/id #uuid "a5e04738-9b21-56d1-b483-703b82e3379a" :question/math-academy-id "e-16707" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,2] \\times [1,2] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^3+y^3-3x-6y+9.\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at $\\mathbf x_0 = \\big[1, \\: 2 \\big]^T$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives $\\mathbf x_1 = \\big[1, \\: 1.5 \\big]^T.$ Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $H(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ and $\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-3x-6y+9) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-3x-6y+9)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}-3 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-6\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2}-6) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}-6)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n![](images/ff/ff3b9d25f1fe5ae3a7f784980aa6bce1340ea9186537f097222fbea82a596257.png)\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{\\begin{bmatrix}6x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}-3 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-6\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}6(1) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6(1.5)\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}3(1)^{2}-3 \\\\ 3(1.5)^{2}-6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0.75\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{6\\cdot 9-0\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0.75\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{54}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4.5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1.417\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203834 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204026 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3db0791-f73d-55e7-9b5a-f8faa62b8820" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method Using Linear Systems"}] :question/id #uuid "e5c61c4c-52a7-52cb-85fe-bc9c3d5e49ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-16708" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times[0,1] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = xy^2+3x^2-xy-x.\n$$\n\nFind the matrix equation that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $\\big[ 1, \\: 1 \\big]^T,$ and compute the components of the first iteration" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $H(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ and $\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nH_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)(\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} - \\mathbf{x}_n) = -\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-xy-x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-xy-x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{2}+6x-y-1 \\\\ 2xy-x\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+6x-y-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+6x-y-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2y-1 \\\\ 2y-1 & 2x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n![](images/dd/ddb94c6d067c419b06cdbec87426e70db6ee07acca7d42c0900ae386f6fe37ae.png)\n\nStarting with ![](images/8b/8bdb55b26b462507d9810675499558be66e7b970d2f9fdb78e708807e617ce8d.png) we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2y-1 \\\\ 2y-1 & 2x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{2}+6x-y-1 \\\\ 2xy-x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2(1)-1 \\\\ 2(1)-1 & 2(1)\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}(1)^{2}+6(1)-(1)-1 \\\\ 2(1)(1)-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get ![](images/da/daee26a8f82011d916bf04d7e374a2b8f471602877fe76335f7f1b242ca86b00.png) Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+\\begin{bmatrix}-9/11 \\\\ -1/11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-9/11 \\\\ -1/11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}0.182 \\\\ 0.909\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204506 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204025 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a19923a-5050-5417-8c83-5fc085a81f0f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "9c1114df-3e50-5262-89b7-9f2dfda31093" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203008} {:answer-field/id #uuid "42402f8f-b70b-5b9a-9b06-9c37c04edfca" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203009}] :question/id #uuid "896f7585-083a-5599-bbcd-1fe65ab748ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-256515" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x, y)$ in the region $[1, 2] \\cdot [- 2, 1]⊆R^{2}$, where\n$f(x, y) = 2x^{2}y + 4x^{2} - 3xy - 8x$.\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at $x_{0} = [1,-2]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives $x_{1} = [2, 0]^{T}$. Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).$x_{2} = [$\n{{field-1}}\n$]$\n{{field-2}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204507 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204025 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a19923a-5050-5417-8c83-5fc085a81f0f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "7439bc04-cb9f-570a-81fa-d62522bde2f4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203010}] :question/id #uuid "e11b3fbb-bc5f-5ec5-b462-27e75571abd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-256520" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x, y)$ in the region $[- 6,-3] \\cdot [1, 2]⊆R^{2}$, where\n$f(x, y) = 2xy^{2} - 6x + 4y^{3} + 7$.\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at $x_{0} = [- 6, 1]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives $x_{1} = [- 3, 2]^{T}$. Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).$x_{2} = [$\n{{field-1}}\n$]$\n$1.75$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0be03329-844c-5231-a459-314a1182f84b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "b752ce65-65dd-5660-834c-feed2d56d700" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203011} {:answer-field/id #uuid "438f0a7a-e28b-5afb-89c3-781a12da3a36" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203012}] :question/id #uuid "10a7e7e7-348e-5afa-aa51-5ee70bac5ed6" :question/math-academy-id "q-256534" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x, y)$ in the region $[1, 2] \\cdot [1, 2]⊆R^{2}$, where\n$f(x, y) = 3xy^{2} + 3x^{2} - 8xy - x$.\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at $x_{0} = [1, 1]^{T}$. Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).$x_{1} = [$\n{{field-1}}\n$]$\n{{field-2}}"}]}
